Q: Need help creating a custom ListBox in C# WinForms (Visual Studio 2022) A: <p>Set the <code>DrawMode</code> to <code>OwnerDrawFixed</code> and override <code>OnDrawItem</code> where you put your drawing logic.</p>
<p>Here is an example of a <code>ListBox</code> displaying font names along with a font example:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class FontFamilyListBox : ListBox
{
    public FontFamilyListBox()
    {
        IntegralHeight = false;
        SelectionMode = SelectionMode.One;
        ItemHeight = Math.Max(ItemHeight, 18);
        Height = 16 * ItemHeight + 3;
        DrawMode = DrawMode.OwnerDrawFixed;

        foreach (var family in FontFamily.Families) {
            Items.Add(family.Name);
        }
    }

    protected override void OnDrawItem(DrawItemEventArgs e)
    {
        const string ExampleText = &quot;Abc 123&quot;;

        string fontFamilyName = (string)Items[e.Index];
        using var itemFont = new Font(fontFamilyName, 12);
        using var brush = new SolidBrush(e.ForeColor);
        e.DrawBackground();
        e.Graphics.DrawString(itemFont.Name, Font, brush, e.Bounds.X, e.Bounds.Y);

        float textWidth = e.Graphics.MeasureString(itemFont.Name, Font).Width;
        float exampleWidth = e.Graphics.MeasureString(ExampleText, itemFont).Width;
        if (textWidth + exampleWidth + 3 &lt; e.Bounds.Width) {
            e.Graphics.DrawString(ExampleText, itemFont, brush, e.Bounds.Right - exampleWidth - 1, e.Bounds.Y);
        }
        e.DrawFocusRectangle();
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/7olsuoqe.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/7olsuoqe.png" alt="enter image description here" /></a>.</p>
<p>This code could be improved by using <code>DrawMode</code> = <code>OwnerDrawVariable</code> and adapting the item height according to the measured height of the font example.</p>
<p>You can find the <a href="https://github.com/OJacot-Descombes/PrettyDocComments/blob/master/PrettyDocComments/CustomOptions/Design/FontFamilyListBox.cs" rel="nofollow noreferrer">original code on my GitHub</a></p>

Q: Set correct default value for color properties A: <p>In Windows Forms, you can use data binding. You have to make a little detour, but in the end this simplifies your code.</p>
<p>For data binding you need a view model. This is a class exposing the data and doing the property notification. This has the advantage, that you can directly work in terms of the logical data (byte and Color values) instead text box or label text values that you have to convert (parse and format).</p>
<p>If several properties represent the same value or an aspect of this value (like the R, G, B components), then one property or field must represent the ground truth. I.e. all other properties use this ground truth property as &quot;backing field&quot;.</p>
<p>View Model exposing the <code>Color</code>, <code>Red</code>, <code>Green</code>, and <code>Blue</code> read-write-properties as well as a read-only property <code>Name</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class RgbColor(Color color) : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    // Ground truth.
    public Color Color
    {
        get;
        set {
            if (field != value) {
                field = value;
                OnPropertyChanged();
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Red));
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Green));
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Blue));
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Name));
            }
        }
    } = color;

    public byte Red
    {
        get =&gt; Color.R;
        set {
            if (value != Color.R) {
                Color = Color.FromArgb(255, value, Color.G, Color.B);
                OnPropertyChanged();
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Color));
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Name));
            }
        }
    }

    public byte Green
    {
        get =&gt; Color.G;
        set {
            if (value != Color.G) {
                Color = Color.FromArgb(255, Color.R, value, Color.B);
                OnPropertyChanged();
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Color));
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Name));
            }
        }
    }

    public byte Blue
    {
        get =&gt; Color.B;
        set {
            if (value != Color.B) {
                Color = Color.FromArgb(255, Color.R, Color.G, value);
                OnPropertyChanged();
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Color));
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(Name));
            }
        }
    }

    public string Name =&gt; Color.IsKnownColor ? Color.Name : Color.ToString();

    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler? PropertyChanged;

    protected void OnPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName] string? propertyName = null)
    {
        PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>After adding this class, it is important to compile the code, to make it visible as data source.</p>
<p>In the user control, add a <code>BindingSource</code> from the toolbox, name it <code>colorBindingSource</code> and set its <code>DataSource</code>. In the <code>DataSource</code> drop-down select <strong>Add new Object Data Source..</strong> and select our <code>RgbColor</code> class. Then use it as <code>DataSource</code>.</p>
<p>Now, select the <code>redTextBox</code> and under <strong>(DataBindings)</strong> set the <code>Text</code> property to <code>colorBindingSource.Red</code>. Then open the <strong>(Advanced)</strong> property and change the Data Source Update Mode from <strong>OnValidation</strong> to <strong>OnPropertyChanged</strong>.</p>
<p>This sounds complicated, but once you are used to it, it goes very smoothly.</p>
<p>Do the same for the other two textboxes with the <code>Green</code> and <code>Blue</code> properties.</p>
<p>In the <code>sampleLabel</code> we can bind two properties: the <code>BackColor</code> and the <code>Text</code> to display the color name. Bind <code>colorBindingSource.Name</code> to <code>Text</code> and also change to <code>OnPropertyChanged</code> (same as for the color components.</p>
<p>For the <code>BackColor</code>, you must open <strong>(Advanced)</strong> and select the <code>BackColor</code> property from the list of all available Label properties. Select <code>colorBindingSource.Color</code> and also change to <code>OnPropertyChanged</code>.</p>
<p>Now, we must adapt the code in the user control. Here is my code:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public partial class RGBColorSelectorUserControl : UserControl
{
    private readonly RgbColor _rgbColor = new(SystemColors.Control);
    private static int[]? _customColors;

    public RGBColorSelectorUserControl()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        colorBindingSource.DataSource = _rgbColor;
    }

    [Browsable(false)]
    [DesignerSerializationVisibility(DesignerSerializationVisibility.Hidden)]
    public byte Red
    {
        get =&gt; _rgbColor.Red;
        set =&gt; _rgbColor.Red = value;
    }

    [Browsable(false)]
    [DesignerSerializationVisibility(DesignerSerializationVisibility.Hidden)]
    public byte Green
    {
        get =&gt; _rgbColor.Green;
        set =&gt; _rgbColor.Green = value;
    }

    [Browsable(false)]
    [DesignerSerializationVisibility(DesignerSerializationVisibility.Hidden)]
    public byte Blue
    {
        get =&gt; _rgbColor.Blue;
        set =&gt; _rgbColor.Blue = value;
    }

    [DefaultValue(typeof(Color), &quot;Control&quot;)]
    public Color SelectedColor
    {
        get =&gt; _rgbColor.Color;
        set {
            if (value != _rgbColor.Color) {
                _rgbColor.Color = value;
                OnSelectedColorChanged();
            }
        }
    }

    public event EventHandler? SelectedColorChanged;

    protected virtual void OnSelectedColorChanged()
    {
        SelectedColorChanged?.Invoke(this, EventArgs.Empty);
    }

    private void SelectColorButton_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        var cd = new ColorDialog {
            Color = SelectedColor,
            FullOpen = true,
            AnyColor = true,
            CustomColors = _customColors
        };
        if (cd.ShowDialog(this) == DialogResult.OK) {
            SelectedColor = cd.Color;
            _customColors = cd.CustomColors;
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>Red</code>, <code>Green</code> and <code>Blue</code> properties are not really necessary, because the <code>SelectedColor</code> exposes <code>R</code>, <code>G</code> and <code>B</code> properties.</p>
<p>Note that I also have a button <code>selectColorButton</code>. Make sure to wire up the <code>Click</code> event handler to <code>SelectColorButton_Click</code> (select it from the drop-down in the properties window switched to the events view).</p>
<p>Compile! The user control appears in the Toolbox window and we can drop it on a form. We don't need any code there. You can select a color for the <code>SelectedColor</code> property in the properties window of the user control, and it will immediately be displayed in the textboxes and in the label (as background color and as text). The default color works as expected in the properties window. I.e., the property name is displayed in bold whenever a color is selected that is not <code>Control</code>.</p>
<p>Whenever the user enters a value in <code>Red</code>, <code>Green</code> or <code>Blue</code>, the new label back color is displayed automatically. When a color is selected from the color dialog the text boxes and the label are automatically updated. Any custom color you add to the <code>Custom Color</code> palette is remembered until you close the application.</p>
